==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|bitūmen}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈbɪt.jʊ.mɪn/|/ˈbɪt͡ʃ.ʊ.mɪn/}}
* {{a|Canada}} {{IPA|en|/ˈbɪt͡ʃ.ʊ.mɪn/|/ˈbɪt.jʊ.mɪn/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/bɪˈtumən/|/bɪˈtjumən/|/baɪˈtumən/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Mélange a trois-bitumen.wav |Audio (UK)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|bitumina|s}}

# A sticky, black, highly viscous liquid or semi-solid form of petroleum, burning with a bright flame. It occurs as an abundant natural product in many places, as on the shores of the Dead and [[Caspian Sea]]s. It is used in [[cement]]s, in the construction of [[pavement]]s, etc.; Mineral [[pitch]].
#: {{syn|en|Jew's pitch}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Jeff Howell|title=Home improvements: gravel paths and cutting heating bills &#91;print version: Cold comfort in technology, 23 August 2014, p.&nbsp;P5&#93;|url=http://www.telegraph.co.uk/property/propertyadvice/11051494/Home-improvements-gravel-paths-and-cutting-heating-bills.html|work={{w|The Daily Telegraph}} (Property)|date=24 August 2014|passage=You need to excavate and remove the topsoil, line the subsoil with a geotextile, then lay and compact hardcore. Follow this with a layer of compacted "hoggin"&nbsp;– compacted clay, gravel and sand. This is then sprayed with hot '''bitumen''', and has a layer of pea shingle rolled into it.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Any one of the natural [[hydrocarbon]]s, including the hard, solid, [[brittle]] varieties called [[asphalt]], the semisolid [[maltha]] and mineral [[tar]]s, the oily [[petrolea]], and even the light, volatile [[naphtha]]s.
# {{lb|en|AU|colloquial}} Roads sealed with bitumen, as opposed to dirt roads.
# {{lb|en|Canada}} Canadian deposits of extremely heavy crude oil.<ref>{{cite-web |title=Archived copy |url=http://www.energy.gov.ab.ca/OilSands/793.asp |accessdate=20 October 2007 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20071020113434/http://www.energy.gov.ab.ca/OilSands/793.asp |archivedate=20 October 2007 }}</ref>

